---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import { referenceScenarios } from '../../data/reference-scenarios';
const usd = (value: number) => new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', { style: 'currency', currency: 'USD', maximumFractionDigits: 0 }).format(value);
---
<ArticleLayout title="Research and calculator methodology" description="How Child Savings Lab verifies rules, labels uncertainty, calculates projections, protects privacy, and keeps comparisons independent." verifiedAt="2026-10-12" effective="Calculator behavior reviewed October 12; source records have individual review dates" sourceIds={['TREASURY-TD-10056','IRS-TRUMP-ACCOUNTS','IRS-FORM-4547-INSTRUCTIONS','IRS-PROPOSED-INVESTMENTS-2026']}>
  <h2>Source hierarchy</h2>
  <p>We begin with statutes, IRS forms and instructions, Treasury or IRS guidance, Federal Register material, and Department of Labor releases. Financial institutions and publishers may inform industry context, but they do not define federal rules.</p>
  <h2>Rule records</h2>
  <p>Each implemented rule has an internal ID, effective dates, a source ID, and a verification date. Rules are not copied into multiple calculators. Proposed rules are stored and presented as proposed.</p>
  <h2>Eligibility logic</h2>
  <p>The checker treats Treasury auto enrollment, account claiming, receiving-account activation, rare manual elections, and the separate pilot contribution election as different states. It returns next steps rather than pretending it can read IRS records. Missing or uncertain required information produces a request for more information, not a negative eligibility decision.</p>
  <p>The new-election age/SSN baseline is not a determination that an existing account is valid or invalid. The account-status path and pilot assessment are independent: incomplete pilot answers affect only that assessment. After the child growth period, the tool directs users to adult IRA rules without requiring them to repeat activation for an account reported as activated.</p>
  <h2>Growth formula</h2>
  <p>The calculator derives the growth-period end from the birth date—December 31 of the year the child turns 17—then compounds monthly. Monthly deposits occur at month-end and annual family or employer deposits at December 31. The selected eligible-fund expense and separate account-fee assumptions are subtracted monthly.</p>
  <p>Later target ages show continued hypothetical growth without post-growth-period IRA contributions. Partial months, taxes, withdrawals, inflation, tracking error, and market sequence are not modeled.</p>
  <p>Family and employer deposits are checked against the current $5,000 benchmark using the actual calendar-year schedule in the model, not twelve times a partial-year monthly amount. Prior and outside contributions are not known to the tool; the opening balance is not treated as a record of current-year deposits. Future inflation adjustments to limits are not predicted. A schedule that passes this check is not confirmation of remaining legal contribution capacity.</p>
  <p>The displayed modeled cutoff is the last eligible month-end within the selected projection, separate from the statutory growth-period end. Annual chart/table snapshots use the same calculation; a final partial-year row does not add partial-month growth. The dashed line includes the starting amount and optional pilot assumption, not a claim about lifetime tax basis.</p>
  <p>Projection starts before July 4, 2026 are rejected. Blank numeric fields are not treated as zero. The sensitivity table uses the selected return and variations of two percentage points, omitting variations outside the supported range. Changing an input hides the previous result until the inputs are validated and calculated again.</p>
  <p>A printed report includes the assumptions, calculation version, and report time. Homepage links can carry an allowlisted, fictional example preset in a URL fragment; birth dates and custom financial inputs are not put in the URL, sent to a server, or saved by this site.</p>
  <h2 id="worked-examples">Reproduce a worked example</h2>
  <p>All three fictional scenarios below use a January 15, 2026 birth date, an October 8, 2026 start, target age 18, no pilot amount, no opening balance, no employer contribution, and no separate account fee. The schedule contains 207 monthly deposits. These are software checks, not independent professional review or predictions.</p>
  <div class="reference-examples" role="region" aria-label="Calculation reference examples" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <caption>Same dates, different contribution and return assumptions</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Scenario</th><th scope="col">Monthly amount</th><th scope="col">Return / fund fee</th><th scope="col">Modeled balance</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{referenceScenarios.map(scenario => <tr><th scope="row">{scenario.label}</th><td>{usd(scenario.familyContribution)}</td><td>{scenario.annualReturnRate}% / {scenario.fundExpenseRate}%</td><td>{usd(scenario.result.endingBalance)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>For the zero-return, zero-fee check, $100 × 207 deposits = $20,700. The other rows include a constant assumed return and the selected fee; actual returns will not follow this constant path. Try the inputs in the <a href="/tools/trump-account-growth-calculator/">Trump Account growth calculator</a> and compare the full report assumptions.</p>
  <h2>Comparison policy</h2>
  <p>Accounts are compared by purpose, eligibility, ownership and control, contribution framework, tax treatment, investment limits, withdrawal rules, and age transitions. No account or provider receives a higher position because of compensation.</p>
  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>If an important rule is wrong or outdated, we update the rule record, affected pages, verification date, and tests together. Contact details are on the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
</ArticleLayout>
